Fixed. The bug was in `extractTags(from:)` in `swift/Sources/NoteKit/NoteKit.swift:5`.

**Root cause:** the old code split the body on spaces (`split(separator: " ")`) then kept tokens starting with `#`. That only handled a space as a delimiter, so any other adjacent character — a comma (`shopping list,#urgent`) or a parenthesis (`(#ideas`) — got glued onto the tag, so the token became `#urgent,` / `#ideas)` and never matched `hasPrefix("#")` cleanly (it did start with `#`, but the trailing punctuation leaked into the extracted name; more importantly the space-only split meant any non-space boundary broke extraction).

**Fix:** replaced the split with a regex scan for `#[\p{L}\p{N}_]+` (leading `#`, then one or more letters/digits/underscores), advancing the search window past each match. This extracts the tag wherever it appears, regardless of surrounding punctuation.

**Verified** (compiled with the project's Swift 6.3.3 via `swiftc` — `swift test` itself fails here due to a sandbox-exec restriction, not the code):
- `hello #world #swift` → `["world","swift"]` (existing test, still passes)
- `just plain text` → `[]` (existing test, still passes)
- `shopping list,#urgent` → `["urgent"]` (new, fixed)
- `(#ideas` → `["ideas"]` (new, fixed)
- order preserved across mixed delimiters; unicode/numeric tags; empty string.

Order of appearance is preserved by construction (left-to-right scan). Existing behavior for space-separated tags is unchanged.


